    "analysis": "Saturday's pulse: the Syrian pound slipped again in the parallel market during the day, with the dollar reaching 149.25 lira (sell) in Damascus by the afternoon, up from 149.00 this morning and 147.25 yesterday. The Central Bank's official rate is unchanged at 122.50 under Bulletin 213, as no new bulletin was published today, so the gap between the two rates widened to about 21.8% — the official rate now buys roughly a fifth less than the street rate. Gold held its morning level: the 21-karat gram stayed at 16,450 lira (sell), up 235 on yesterday, while the global ounce was almost flat near $3,874, so the rise in gold is a local-currency story, not a world-market one. Nearly every currency edged up about a quarter of a percent in parallel terms. For savers, the message is unchanged: gold holders gained in lira terms, but dollars cost more than yesterday.",
